# goal Prove the wire, not the wiring

The whole test suite runs against this: a genuine ACP agent over genuine stdio,
built on the same SDK. Mocking the connection would only prove the code calls the
functions it was written to call. Rationale in [_.hint#fixture](../../_.hint).

# rule Extend the fixture, never reach for a mocking library {#no_mocks}

A new behaviour to test is a new branch here, keyed off the prompt text. That keeps
every test honest about the protocol and keeps the suite offline and free — it
spends no subscription and needs no network.

The one exception is the HTTP layer, where `test/server.test.js` may inject a stub
agent object to test routing that has nothing to do with ACP.

# rule Prompt keywords {#keywords}

The prompt text selects the behaviour, so a test asks for the failure it wants.

| keyword | behaviour | what it exists to prove |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `QUOTA` | fails with usage-limit phrasing | the 429 path |
| `BOOM` | fails with an unrelated error | that 502 stays 502 |
| `DIE` | exits the child during `session/prompt` without answering | mid-turn crash classification and respawn |
| `BOOM_AFTER_OUTPUT` | emits a thought, then fails with an unrelated error | post-header SSE error framing |
| `SPLIT_STOP` | emits `STOP` across two text chunks | streaming stop-prefix holdback |
| `HANG` | emits identifiable partial text, then never finishes on its own | cancellation, the 504 path, and partial-answer honesty |
| `IGNORE_CANCEL` | keeps streaming after `session/cancel` and never answers the prompt | the post-cancel drain deadline and dead-session rule |
| `HANG_CLOSE` | answers normally, then leaves that session's `session/close` request pending | close is bounded, logged, and swallowed |
| `HANG_AFTER_TOOL` | calls a client tool, accepts its result, then never finishes on its own | resumed-turn timeout and disconnect handling |
| `DELAY_AFTER_TOOL` | calls a client tool, accepts its result, then waits 250 ms before answering | deterministic concurrent tool-result ownership |
| `DELAY_RESUME` | marks the session so its next `session/resume` waits 100 ms | deterministically opens the parked-continuation race window |
| `LATE_RESULT` | calls a client tool initially; a replay containing its result answers with `FRESH:<session id>` | late results restart from caller history on a new session |
| `REFUSE` | completes with `stopReason: "refusal"` | stop-reason mapping |
| `COUNT` | streams 20 words, checking the abort each one | `max_tokens` and `stop` |
| `INTERLEAVE` | alternates 20 reasoning/text pairs | stream finalization, item ordering and stored-byte admission |
| `TELEMETRY` | reports context `{used: 1, size: 3}` and cumulative USD cost in 1.25 increments | response annotations and per-turn cost settling |
| `LONG_OUTPUT` | streams 256 chunks of 8 KiB over real ACP stdio | slow-reader HTTP backpressure measurement |
| `ECHOMCP` | answers with the `mcpServers` it received | that MCP config reaches `session/new` |
| `ECHOSESSION` | answers with its own session id | which session a turn actually ran in |
| `TEXT_TOOL_CALL` | answers `submit_plan({"x":1})` without calling the served tool | text imitation diagnostics never fabricate a call |
| anything else | echoes after a thought chunk and a tool call | the ordinary path |

Adding a keyword means adding a row here. A branch nobody can find from this table
is a branch the next person will duplicate.

# rule Pre-session and process fixture modes

Failures before a prompt exists are selected through the fixture environment:

| variable | behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| `HANG_INITIALIZE_ONCE=<marker>` | the first child to create the marker never answers `initialize`; respawns answer normally |
| `HANG_RPC=session/new` | `session/new` never answers |
| `RPC_CAPTURE_FILE=<path>` | writes the first MCP URL received by `session/new`, so cleanup can be probed over real HTTP |
| `PID_FILE=<path>` | writes the fixture pid at startup |
| `WARM_CAPTURE_FILE=<path>` | appends `warm` for `WARMCOUNT` prompts and `fork` for fork requests, across respawns |
| `TRAP_SIGTERM=1` | ignores SIGTERM so shutdown must escalate to SIGKILL |
| `GARBAGE_STDOUT=1` | writes non-JSON lines before initialization and during a prompt, among valid frames |
| `LONG_CAPTURE_FILE=<path>` | marks when a `LONG_OUTPUT` turn has emitted all 2 MiB, before the HTTP reader resumes |
| `NO_CONFIG_OPTIONS=1` | returns no config options from `session/new` |
| `PROBE_GUARD=1` | fails if a probe sends `session/set_config_option` or `session/prompt` |
| `SET_CONFIG_CAPTURE_FILE=<path>` | appends `<configId>=<value>` for every `session/set_config_option` request |
| `ANTHROPIC_MODEL=<value>` | reports that value as the initial model `currentValue`, mirroring Claude's process-level selector |

Keep these modes environmental: no prompt or session exists when initialize and
process shutdown behaviour must be selected.

# invariant A session rejects overlapping prompts {#no_overlapping_prompts}

The fixture throws `overlapping session/prompt on <session id>` if a second prompt
arrives before the first settles. This is deliberately loud: real codex deadlocks
in that situation, so every concurrency test must fail at the wire boundary rather
than merely time out or accept crossed output.

# rule The option set must stay unstable {#unstable_options}

Selecting model `lite` **removes** the `thought_level` selector, mirroring what
`claude-agent-acp` does for Haiku. That behaviour is the point of the model existing;
do not simplify it away. See [_.hint#option_reapply](../../_.hint) — the live run
caught that bug precisely because the fixture was stable at the time.

Likewise the reasoning option is named `effort`, not `reasoning`, so that selection
by category is genuinely exercised rather than accidentally matching an id.

# invariant Session ids are monotonic, never reused {#unique_ids}

Derived from a counter, not from the live session count. With ids that come round
again after a close, a test asserting "this is a *different* session" passes by
accident — which is exactly what happened while the continuity tests were written.

# invariant Every prompt turn is cancellable {#cancellable}

`state.abort` is created at the start of each turn, not only for `HANG`. Any
ordinary long-running branch must check it, or a cancellation test hangs until the
suite times out instead of failing. `IGNORE_CANCEL` is the named exception: it is
the deliberately broken child used to prove the production drain deadline.

# invariant The fixture advertises what it implements

`sessionCapabilities.close` is declared and `session/close` is handled, so the
production close path is exercised rather than skipped. If a branch stops being
implemented, stop advertising it — a capability the agent claims and does not honour
is exactly the bug this fixture should be catching.

Forked state records the `mcpServers` on the fork request rather than inheriting the
parent's field by accident. `ECHOMCP` can therefore distinguish a truthful fork
declaration from a fixture that merely hid a missing one.
